## The layers

App Router changes the state picture because most data-fetching now happens on the server. Instead of one global store for everything, the question becomes: where does each piece of state actually live?

```
Server state         → Server Components, fetch + cache
URL state            → searchParams, router.push
Form state           → useActionState (server actions) or useState
Local UI state       → useState, useReducer
Shared client state  → Context or Zustand / Jotai
```

## Server state — keep it on the server

For data fetched from an API or database, the best state manager is the server itself:

```tsx
// app/products/page.tsx — Server Component
export default async function ProductsPage() {
  const products = await db.product.findMany();
  // No useState, no useEffect, no client state
  return <ProductGrid products={products} />;
}
```

Revalidation replaces polling or cache invalidation on the client:

```ts
// When a product changes
revalidateTag("products");
```

## URL state — underused but powerful

Filters, sort order, pagination, and search terms belong in the URL — they're shareable, bookmarkable, and survive a page refresh:

```tsx
"use client";
import { useSearchParams, useRouter, usePathname } from "next/navigation";

export function FilterBar() {
  const searchParams = useSearchParams();
  const router = useRouter();
  const pathname = usePathname();

  function setFilter(key: string, value: string) {
    const params = new URLSearchParams(searchParams.toString());
    params.set(key, value);
    router.push(`${pathname}?${params.toString()}`);
  }

  return (
    <select
      value={searchParams.get("category") ?? ""}
      onChange={(e) => setFilter("category", e.target.value)}
    >
      <option value="">All</option>
      <option value="books">Books</option>
    </select>
  );
}
```

## Local UI state — useState

Standard React. Works the same in Next.js — just make sure the component is marked `"use client"`:

```tsx
"use client";

export function Accordion({ title, children }) {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setOpen((v) => !v)}>{title}</button>
      {open && <div>{children}</div>}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Shared client state — Context

For state shared across a subtree, wrap with a Context provider in a `"use client"` component and mount it in a Server Component layout:

```tsx
// app/providers.tsx
"use client";

const CartContext = createContext<CartStore | null>(null);

export function CartProvider({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const [items, setItems] = useState<CartItem[]>([]);
  return (
    <CartContext.Provider value={{ items, setItems }}>
      {children}
    </CartContext.Provider>
  );
}

// app/layout.tsx — Server Component
import { CartProvider } from "./providers";

export default function RootLayout({ children }) {
  return (
    <html>
      <body>
        <CartProvider>{children}</CartProvider>
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

## Global client state — Zustand

For state that's shared across many unrelated components (toast notifications, theme, shopping cart) and where Context would require a deeply nested provider:

```ts
// stores/cart.ts
import { create } from "zustand";

type CartStore = {
  items: CartItem[];
  addItem: (item: CartItem) => void;
  removeItem: (id: string) => void;
};

export const useCartStore = create<CartStore>((set) => ({
  items: [],
  addItem: (item) => set((s) => ({ items: [...s.items, item] })),
  removeItem: (id) =>
    set((s) => ({ items: s.items.filter((i) => i.id !== id) })),
}));
```

Use it anywhere in a `"use client"` component without a provider:

```tsx
"use client";
import { useCartStore } from "@/stores/cart";

export function AddToCartButton({ product }) {
  const addItem = useCartStore((s) => s.addItem);
  return <button onClick={() => addItem(product)}>Add to cart</button>;
}
```

## Redux — when it makes sense

For large teams with complex state, time-travel debugging needs, or existing Redux codebases, Redux Toolkit still works in Next.js. The extra setup (custom App provider, server-side hydration with `next-redux-wrapper`) is the cost. For greenfield App Router projects, Zustand covers 90% of Redux use cases at 10% of the boilerplate.

