## The core problem with SPAs

A plain React SPA sends this:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <body>
    <div id="root"></div>
    <script src="/bundle.js"></script>
  </body>
</html>
```

Googles bot fetches the page, sees an empty div, and has to wait for JavaScript to execute before indexing content. Googlebot does render JavaScript, but it's slower and subject to crawl budget — it might index stale content or miss pages entirely.

Next.js sends:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>Butlin's Skegness — Summer Breaks 2025</title>
    <meta property="og:title" content="Butlin's Skegness" />
    <!-- ... -->
  </head>
  <body>
    <article>
      <h1>Summer Breaks from £299</h1>
      <p>Book your family holiday...</p>
    </article>
  </body>
</html>
```

Everything a crawler needs, without waiting for JS.

## The Metadata API

Next.js merges metadata from every layout and page in the tree:

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx — global defaults
export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: { template: "%s | Acme", default: "Acme" },
  description: "Enterprise software",
  metadataBase: new URL("https://acme.com"),
};

// app/products/[slug]/page.tsx — page-specific
export async function generateMetadata({ params }: Props): Promise<Metadata> {
  const { slug } = await params;
  const product = await getProduct(slug);
  return {
    title: product.name,
    description: product.description,
    openGraph: {
      title: product.name,
      images: [{ url: product.image }],
      type: "website",
    },
    alternates: {
      canonical: `/products/${slug}`,
    },
  };
}
```

This generates correct `<title>`, `<meta>`, `<link rel="canonical">`, and OG tags in one place — no forgetting to wire up a Head component per page.

## Structured data (JSON-LD)

For rich search results (product prices, review stars, event dates), include JSON-LD via a script tag:

```tsx
export default async function ProductPage({ params }: Props) {
  const { id } = await params;
  const product = await getProduct(id);

  const jsonLd = {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Product",
    name: product.name,
    image: product.image,
    offers: {
      "@type": "Offer",
      price: product.price,
      priceCurrency: "GBP",
    },
  };

  return (
    <>
      <script
        type="application/ld+json"
        dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: JSON.stringify(jsonLd) }}
      />
      <ProductView product={product} />
    </>
  );
}
```

## Sitemap and robots.txt

```ts
// app/sitemap.ts
export default async function sitemap(): Promise<MetadataRoute.Sitemap> {
  const products = await db.product.findMany({ select: { slug: true, updatedAt: true } });
  return [
    { url: "https://acme.com", lastModified: new Date() },
    ...products.map((p) => ({
      url: `https://acme.com/products/${p.slug}`,
      lastModified: p.updatedAt,
    })),
  ];
}

// app/robots.ts
export default function robots(): MetadataRoute.Robots {
  return {
    rules: [{ userAgent: "*", allow: "/", disallow: "/admin" }],
    sitemap: "https://acme.com/sitemap.xml",
  };
}
```

## Core Web Vitals as ranking signals

Google's Page Experience update made LCP, CLS, and INP ranking factors. The Next.js defaults directly improve each:

- **LCP** — `next/image` with `priority` on the hero; Server Components reducing JS parse time
- **CLS** — `next/image` reserves space; `next/font` with size-adjusted fallbacks
- **INP** — Server Components mean less JS to execute on interaction

## Incremental Static Regeneration for freshness

SSG with `revalidate` means pages are pre-rendered (fast, crawlable) but updated in the background. The crawler always gets rendered HTML, never waits for JS, and always sees recent content:

```ts
export const revalidate = 3600; // Re-render at most once per hour
```

